NOTEBOOKS["41_budget_placebo"].append((M, """### The members' own draft budgetary plans as a second reference
The Commission's forecast is the outside view; the government's own plan is the inside one. `sources/dbp_euro.py` downloads the English copies of the October draft budgetary plans of the euro-area members from the Commission's yearly pages (119 files, 2018-2025) and reads the planned net lending of the general government from the macro table: three B.9 rows whose values add up (rest of the world = private sector + general government) find the right row whatever its label. 51 plans are verified this way (9 countries: Austria, Cyprus, Greece, Italy, Lithuania, Latvia, Malta, the Netherlands, Portugal; 4-9 a year), ten more are label-only and left out, 58 are not parsed (new templates, fractions, wrapped labels). Italy's eight parsed plans match `deficit_targets.csv`. Three identity-only rows of 2021 that differ from the Commission's forecast of that autumn by 2.4-6.3 points are dropped as misreadings. The sample is thin, so each rank is shown next to the Commission-based rank for exactly the same country-years.
""")); NOTEBOOKS["41_budget_placebo"].append((C, '''dp = xres["x12_dbp_plans"]; dr = xres["x13_dbp_ranks"]
print("plans used (country-years):", len(dp), "| countries:", sorted(dp.iso3.unique()), "| plans per year:", dp.groupby("year").size().to_dict())
print("Italy's plan and surprise against it by year (net lending, % of GDP):"); display(dp[dp.iso3.eq("ITA")].round(2))
print("Italy's rank among the countries with plans (1 = the largest overrun), own plan against the Commission forecast on the same country-years:"); display(dr.drop(columns="countries").round(2))
'''))
NOTEBOOKS["41_budget_placebo"].append((M, """## Takeaways (deficit surprise against the Commission's autumn forecast, 27 members, budget years 2017-2025; descriptive)
- **Data.** The statistical annexes of the ten autumn forecasts of 2016-2025 give net lending, revenue, expenditure and interest for all 27 members (the autumn 2015 annex has the old layout and is read separately: it adds budget year 2016, used only as a sensitivity window, so the primary windows start in 2017). Every member has a surprise in every year, and the three parts add up to the deficit surprise within 0.04 points on average (largest gap 0.2: rounding of the published figures; Denmark's total revenue of 2024 and 2025 is missing from Eurostat's release of 21 October 2026, so the revenue windows that contain those years rank 26 members). As a check of the parsing, the estimate column of each annex agrees with the latest Eurostat outturn within 0.08 to 0.31 points on average.
- **Adding budget year 2016 changes nothing.** With the autumn 2015 annex as a sensitivity window (2016-25 without 2020, not primary, no q) Italy is second of 27 on the deficit surprise (raw +0.53, median of the others -0.72; p 0.074), as in the primary window; Italy's own surprise in 2016 is +0.10 against a median of -0.85 for the others (rank 5).
- **Italy's deficit overrun of 2021-23 was the largest of the 27.** Against the autumn forecast of the year before, the outturn deficit was above the forecast by 2.30 points of GDP on average (median of the other 26: -1.25); minus the other members' yearly median it is 3.58 (the next are Hungary 2.88 and Latvia 2.28). The one-sided placebo p is 1/27 = 0.037, the smallest the design allows, and it does not survive false-discovery control: over the 16 pre-specified tests (two windows, four components, two measures) the smallest q is 0.254 and none reaches 0.05. Among the 20 euro-area members Italy is also first (p 0.05).
- **The rank does not hinge on one year or one horizon.** In 2022 and 2023 taken singly Italy is first (+2.3 and +3.5 against medians of -1.25 and -0.85 for the others; 2021 is third, +1.1). Leaving one year out of 2021-23 gives rank 2 (2021-22) or 1 (2022-23, 2021 and 2023). Against the forecast of the budget year itself the rank is still first (1.43 points); against the forecast of two years before it is second (3.70).
- **The result does not depend on the outturn vintage.** With the Commission's own estimate of the year just ended (autumn forecast of B+1, budget years 2017-24) instead of the latest Eurostat figures, 2021-23 is still first of 27 (raw +1.73, median of the others -1.05; idiosyncratic +3.02) and the period without 2020 still second (raw +0.47). What the later revisions changed is mostly one number: Italy's 2021 deficit was revised up by 1.7 points of GDP (the other members' median -0.1, largest +0.2), which is the accounting treatment of the building credits; in every other year Italy's revision is within 0.2.
- **A GDP forecast error does not explain it; at constant GDP it is larger.** Rebuilding forecast nominal GDP from the forecast growth and deflator, the denominator term is negative in Italy in 2021-23 (-0.47, -0.30, -0.17: inflation made the economy larger than forecast, which lowers every ratio), so the miss of the deficit in euro at the outturn GDP is +1.57, +2.60 and +3.67 points; for 2021-23 Italy is first on that level miss (raw 2.62, idiosyncratic 3.64, p 0.037) and the denominator term itself ranks 21 of 27. Without 2020 the whole period is also first on the level miss (raw 0.71, p 0.037) where the plain surprise is second (0.59). The forecast GDP is a rebuilt level, not a published one (latest outturn of B-2 times forecast growth), so these figures carry that approximation.\n- **Outside those years Italy is unremarkable.** 2017-19 rank 10 (raw -0.30, median of the others -0.50), 2024-25 rank 15. Without 2020 the whole period gives rank 2 (0.59 raw, 1.42 idiosyncratic; p 0.074, q 0.254): the 2021-23 years carry it. In 2020 the pandemic overrun was 7.1 points, rank 8 (median of the others 5.65).
- **It is spread over the parts, not one of them.** In 2021-23 the idiosyncratic means are 1.68 for the revenue shortfall (rank 5 of 27), 2.15 for primary expenditure (rank 3) and 0.28 for interest (rank 3); none of them is first, and none has a q below 0.25. Other members have larger single parts (Latvia 4.05 and Austria 2.92 on primary expenditure; Bulgaria 2.52 on revenue), so what is unusual in Italy is the sum. This is compatible with the Italian account of notebook 38 (capital transfers for the building credits) but does not test it: no other country has that item here, and the outturn is the latest Eurostat vintage, whose treatment of the credits was revised, so part of the surprise is accounting.
- **The two references agree for Italy.** The Commission's deficit forecast was on average 0.13 points above the government's own target (0.03 in 2021-23; the largest gaps +0.8 in 2021 and -0.9 in 2023), so the miss is about the same against either (2021-23: +1.1, +2.3, +3.5 against the Commission, +1.9, +2.5, +2.6 against the government).
- **The country screen cannot discover anything by design; it only orders.** The sign-flip screen of the mean idiosyncratic surprise (2017-25 without 2020) finds 0 of 27 discoveries, but with 8 years the smallest attainable p is about 0.008 and the smallest q over 27 countries about 0.11 (reached by Denmark, -2.42, and the Netherlands, -1.02, which tie): a real persistent difference could not pass either. The order is still informative: Hungary +1.53 (p 0.016, q 0.14) first and Italy second (+1.42, p 0.112, q 0.32), with Denmark and the Netherlands at the bottom (deficits smaller than forecast). The sign-randomised null gives 0 discoveries in 200 runs and 4.6% of p-values below 0.05, so the p-values are calibrated even if the screen has no power at this size.
- **The governments' own plans give the same placement, on a thin sample.** From 51 verified draft budgetary plans of 9 euro-area members (4-9 a year), Italy's 2021-23 deficit exceeded its own plan by 2.70 points on average (4.03 against the median of the others in the same year): first of 8 countries (p 1/8; the Commission-based figure on the same country-years is 2.30, first of 8 too). Over 2018-25 without 2020 it is 0.96 (1.86 idiosyncratic), first of 7; the Commission-based rank on those country-years is also first on both measures. Italy's 2023 plan is the outgoing government's October one (-3.4; the November update said -4.5, which would shorten the 2023 surprise from 3.7 to 2.6). No test is possible with 7-8 countries (the smallest p is 1/8) and the countries with parsable plans are not a random draw: this is a second look, not a confirmation. The plan table: `data/external/dbp_targets.csv`.\n- **Limits.** One-sided ranks among 27 cannot go below 0.037 and Italy is one country, so this is a placement, not a calibrated test; the 16 tests are strongly dependent (BH is conservative here). The reference is the Commission's forecast (policy as known in the autumn), not each government's plan; ratios are in % of GDP and a GDP error moves them (separated in the denominator split, which rests on a forecast GDP level rebuilt from the latest outturn of B-2 and the forecast growth, not a published level); Cyprus 2018 and similar one-offs are in the sample; ten years, with 2020 and the 2021-23 recovery dominating. All associations are descriptive: a forecast error is not a policy effect.
"""))
